.Nd named process registry
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc_reg.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_reg_create "xtc_reg_t **out"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_reg_destroy "xtc_reg_t *r"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_reg_put "xtc_reg_t *r" "const char *name" "xtc_pid_t pid" "void *value" "unsigned flags"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_reg_get "xtc_reg_t *r" "const char *name" "xtc_pid_t *out_pid"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_reg_del "xtc_reg_t *r" "const char *name" "xtc_pid_t pid"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_reg_visit "xtc_reg_t *r" "const char *name" "xtc_reg_visit_fn fn" "void *user" "int *out_n"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_reg_members "xtc_reg_t *r" "const char *key" "int (*fn)(xtc_pid_t, void *)" "void *user"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_reg_count "const xtc_reg_t *r"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_reg_drop_pid "xtc_reg_t *r" "xtc_pid_t pid"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_reg_reaper "void *reg"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_svr_call_name "xtc_reg_t *r" "const char *name" "const void *req" "size_t req_size" "void **out_reply" "size_t *out_size" "int64_t timeout_ns"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_reg_whereis_wait "xtc_reg_t *r" "const char *name" "xtc_pid_t not_pid" "int64_t timeout_ns" "xtc_pid_t *out_pid"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_reg_send_wait "xtc_reg_t *r" "const char *name" "const void *data" "size_t size" "int64_t timeout_ns"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_reg
is a name-to-pid registry.  Modeled on Erlang's
.Sq register/2
process registration.  Lets a process find a peer by canonical
name without sharing pid pointers ahead of time.
.Pp
Names are strings.
xtc_app owns a registry per app
.Pq Xr xtc_app_registry 3 .
Multiple registries are permitted for sharded namespaces.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_reg_put
adds an entry (see
.Sx Entries
below).
.Fn xtc_reg_get
looks up the pid bound to
.Fa name
.Pq the Erlang Sq whereis ,
writing it to
.Fa out_pid ;
for a group key it reports the most recent member.
It returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
when
.Fa name
has no entry.
Under the simulator a lookup may report a transient miss, the outcome a
racing removal can also produce; callers that loop retry.
.Fn xtc_reg_del
removes one entry: with
.Fa pid
.Dv XTC_PID_NONE
the most recent entry under
.Fa name
(for a unique name, its only one), otherwise the entry for
.Pq Fa name , Fa pid ,
such as one member leaving a group.
It returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
when nothing matches.
.Fn xtc_reg_count
returns the number of live entries in
.Fa r .
.Fn xtc_reg_drop_pid
removes
.Fa pid
from EVERY key it appears under (unique names and all duplicate-key
groups) and returns the number of entries removed -- the "process left
everything" cleanup an embedder calls when a process exits or a
connection closes.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_reg_reaper
is the CRASH-AWARE registry: a process with this body and the registry
as its argument registers itself, then monitors every pid put with
.Dv XTC_REG_MONITOR
and calls
.Fn xtc_reg_drop_pid
automatically when such a pid goes DOWN -- the automatic form of the
manual cleanup.
.Xr xtc_app_start 3
runs one for
.Fn xtc_app_registry
and stops it with the root supervisor.  For a registry of your own,
spawn one
.Pq Fn xtc_proc_spawn loop xtc_reg_reaper reg ...
or make it a supervised child.  It runs until it is killed or receives
.Dv XTC_APP_SHUTDOWN_MSG ;
it parks meanwhile, so a plain
.Xr xtc_loop_run 3
of its loop does not return until one of those happens.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_svr_call_name
is via-dispatch: it looks up
.Fa name No -> No pid and then Fn xtc_svr_call No it,
letting a client address a gen_server by registered name instead of
holding its pid.  It returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
if the name is not registered.
.Fn xtc_reg_members
visits every pid registered under
.Fa key ;
the callback runs UNDER the registry lock (keep it brief; do not
re-enter the registry), a nonzero return stops the walk, and the return
value is the number of members visited.
.Ss Entries
.Fn xtc_reg_put
registers
.Fa name
to
.Fa pid
and an opaque, caller-owned
.Fa value
(the registry stores it and never dereferences or frees it).
With
.Fa flags
0 the name is unique and a taken name is
.Dv XTC_E_EXIST ;
.Dv XTC_REG_DUP
adds a duplicate-key member: many pids may share one key, so a key
names a GROUP, the substrate for pub/sub and process groups
.Pq Xr xtc_pg 3 ;
putting the same key and pid again is a no-op that keeps the first
value.
.Dv XTC_REG_MONITOR
also drops the entry when
.Fa pid
goes DOWN.
It needs a running reaper; with none it is a plain put.
When a reaper IS running, the put either establishes monitoring or
fails: if the enrollment cannot be delivered to the reaper (its mailbox
is bounded and may be full) the entry is rolled back and the call
returns
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
.Pq or Dv XTC_E_RESOURCE ,
so a caller never holds a monitored name that nothing is watching.
Retry, or put without the flag and call
.Fn xtc_reg_drop_pid
yourself.
If the reaper itself cannot establish the monitor, it drops the pid's
names at once rather than leave them to outlive it.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_reg_visit
calls
.Fa fn Ns Pq Fa pid , value , user
for each entry under
.Fa name
with the registry lock held for the whole walk.
That is the lifetime rule: an owner that removes its entry before it
frees the value cannot free it while
.Fa fn
runs, because the removal waits for the lock.
.Fa fn
must therefore be brief, must not park, and must not call into the
registry (it would deadlock);
.Fn xtc_proc_wake
and atomic stores are fine.
A nonzero return stops the walk.
It returns
.Dv XTC_OK
if at least one entry was visited and
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
if there was none; a miss is authoritative (unlike
.Fn xtc_reg_get ,
the simulator never fakes one).
.Fa out_n ,
if not NULL, gets the number visited.
There is deliberately no call that returns the value: once the lock is
dropped the value may already be freed.
.Pp
The pattern this serves, a stable id that reaches a live control struct
and flips a bit in it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static int
interrupt_fn(xtc_pid_t pid, void *value, void *user)
{
	struct backend *b = value;
	xtc_atomic_fetch_or_u32(&b->pending, *(uint32_t *)user,
	    xtc_mo_release);
	(void)xtc_proc_wake(pid);
	return 0;
}

/* owner, at start:  xtc_reg_put(reg, "backend:42", xtc_self(), b, 0);
 * owner, at exit:   xtc_reg_del(reg, "backend:42", XTC_PID_NONE); then free b */
uint32_t bit = INTERRUPT_CANCEL;
rc = xtc_reg_visit(reg, "backend:42", interrupt_fn, &bit, NULL);
.Ed
.Ss Waiting for a name
.Fn xtc_reg_whereis_wait ,
added in 2.2, parks until
.Fa name
names a live process other than
.Fa not_pid
.Pq Dv XTC_PID_NONE No for any ,
then stores it in
.Fa *out_pid .
It is woken by the registration itself (an insert under the name, or a
supervisor giving a child's name to its next incarnation), not by a
timer, so a client whose server died picks up the restarted one as soon
as it is registered:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_pid_t eng;
if (xtc_reg_whereis_wait(reg, "engine", dead_pid, 5000000000LL,
    &eng) == XTC_OK)
	xtc_send(eng, &req, sizeof req);
.Ed
.Pp
A name that still names a dead process does not satisfy the wait.  The
lookup is exact: unlike
.Fn xtc_reg_get
it never reports a simulated transient miss.  From a process it parks
the fiber, and a waiter that is killed is unlinked from the registry on
the way out; from a plain thread it blocks the thread.
.Fa timeout_ns
< 0 waits forever, 0 looks once.  It returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
.Er XTC_E_AGAIN
when the time ran out,
.Er XTC_E_INVAL
for a NULL argument,
.Er XTC_E_NOMEM
/
.Er XTC_E_RESOURCE
if the wait could not be set up, or
.Er XTC_E_STATE
where it would wait on the simulator's own thread outside a process
(the delivery function of
.Xr xtc_sim_external_complete 3 ,
which no registration can reach while it waits).
.Pp
.Fn xtc_reg_send_wait ,
also added in 2.2, sends
.Fa data
to whatever live process
.Fa name
names: it waits as
.Fn xtc_reg_whereis_wait
does while the name is unregistered, and as
.Xr xtc_send_wait 3
does while that process's mailbox is full.  A holder that dies before
or during the send is skipped and the next one waited for.
.Dv XTC_OK
means the message is in a live incarnation's mailbox; one that dies
before taking it loses it with its mailbox, as for any message.
.Er XTC_E_AGAIN
means
.Fa timeout_ns
(one deadline for the whole call) ran out, and
.Er XTC_E_STATE
is a wait it cannot make, as for
.Fn xtc_reg_whereis_wait
and
.Xr xtc_send_wait 3 .
It is the call a device or I/O thread uses to report to a supervised
service that may be restarting.
.Pp
The supervisor names a child itself when its spec sets
.Va reg_name
.Pq Xr xtc_supervisor 3 ;
an
.Xr xtc_app 3
child is named in
.Fn xtc_app_registry .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Dv XTC_OK
on success;
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a NULL argument or unknown
.Fa flags ;
.Dv XTC_E_EXIST
when
.Fn xtc_reg_put
finds a unique name taken;
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
when
.Fn xtc_reg_get ,
.Fn xtc_reg_del
or
.Fn xtc_reg_visit
find no entry;
.Dv XTC_E_NOMEM
on allocation failure.
The waits are described above.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_reg_t *r = xtc_app_registry(app);
xtc_pid_t pid;

/* Bind a name to my process. */
xtc_reg_put(r, "metrics_proc", xtc_self(), NULL, 0);

/* From elsewhere: */
if (xtc_reg_get(r, "metrics_proc", &pid) == XTC_OK)
    xtc_send(pid, msg, sz);
.Ed
.Sh DEPRECATED
These names still work through the rest of 2.x and are
removed in 3.0; see
.Lk https://codeberg.org/gregburd/libxtc/src/branch/main/docs/deprecations.md docs/deprecations.md .
A rebuild that uses one warns.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn xtc_reg_register
Use
.Fn xtc_reg_put ;
call it as xtc_reg_put(r, name, pid, NULL, 0); a taken name is XTC_E_EXIST (the old name: XTC_E_INVAL).
.It Fn xtc_reg_register_dup
Use
.Fn xtc_reg_put ;
call it as xtc_reg_put(r, key, pid, NULL, XTC_REG_DUP).
.It Fn xtc_reg_register_mon
Use
.Fn xtc_reg_put ;
call it as xtc_reg_put(r, name, pid, NULL, XTC_REG_MONITOR).
.It Fn xtc_reg_unregister
Use
.Fn xtc_reg_del ;
call it as xtc_reg_del(r, name, XTC_PID_NONE); a miss is XTC_E_NOTFOUND (the old name: XTC_E_INVAL).
.It Fn xtc_reg_unregister_pid
Use
.Fn xtc_reg_del ;
same arguments; a miss is XTC_E_NOTFOUND (the old name: XTC_E_INVAL).
.It Fn xtc_reg_whereis
Use
.Fn xtc_reg_get ;
same arguments; a miss is XTC_E_NOTFOUND (the old name: XTC_E_INVAL).
.El
